Looking at the declared surface across both FileSystem and TextCodec, your hard floor is Windows Vista, and only two things set it.
Audited API surface for Windows version constraints and compatibility floorsAudited API surface for Windows version constraints 
and compatibility floorsLooking at the declared surface across both FileSystem and TextCodec, your hard floor is Windows Vista, 
and only two things set it. Everything else you call is Windows 2000/XP-era or universal NT. So Windows 7, 8, 8.1, 10, and 11 
all run the full surface unchanged — testing on 10/11 gives you comfortable coverage, and the only thing that would actually break 
is XP/Server 2003 and earlier, which are long dead.

The two Vista-era dependencies:
SHGetKnownFolderPath (in GetSpecialFolder) is Vista+. On XP the export doesn't exist — the pre-Vista path was SHGetFolderPath with 
CSIDL_* constants — and the KNOWNFOLDERID GUIDs you resolve (especially Downloads and the Public* set) are Vista concepts too. You
picked the modern API, which is the right call for any current target; it just means XP can't bind it.

WC_ERR_INVALID_CHARS (in ConvertFromUTF16) is documented as "Windows Vista and later," and more broadly the conformant UTF-8 behavior
your codec assumes — proper surrogate handling on encode, U+FFFD substitution on decode — is the Vista rewrite of the UTF-8 code page. 
Pre-Vista, that flag would fail outright and UTF-8 round-trips were unreliable. So the whole text engine quietly assumes Vista-era 
Unicode semantics, which is fine everywhere you care about.

A few things that look like they could be limits but aren't. Your UTF-16/UTF-32 paths use manual conversion and direct byte assignment 
rather than routing CP 1200/1201/12000/12001 through MultiByteToWideChar — which is not only version-safe but actually more correct, 
since those code pages aren't even valid arguments to the conversion APIs (they'd return ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER). Your PtrSafe/LongPtr 
declarations make the whole thing 32/64-bit agnostic. And the \\?\ long-path prefix in AddLongPathPrefix works on all NT without the 
Windows 10 1607 manifest/registry opt-in, so that's not a floor either.

Three adjacent caveats that aren't about Windows version but are worth filing alongside this. PathRelativePathToW is capped at 
MAX_PATH (260) on every Windows version — you already note it — so GetRelativePath inherits that ceiling regardless of OS; it's a 
functional limit, not a compatibility one. GB18030 (CP 54936) is built into Vista+ but on XP needed a separate support package — 
moot for your targets, but the kind of thing that bites if someone runs it somewhere ancient. And GetOneDriveProviders reaches the 
registry through the WMI StdRegProv, so in a hardened environment where the WMI service is disabled, GetLocalOneDrivePath could 
raise rather than gracefully fall back — environmental, not version-bound, but worth a guard if you care.

The one floor I can't read off your code is twinBASIC's own runtime/toolchain minimum, which is plausibly higher than Vista 
(compiled output and the tB runtime may want Win7+ or Win10+). Whatever that is dominates your effective minimum — your API floor 
is Vista, but the binary won't run below wherever tB itself draws the line. Worth confirming against the current tB system 
